WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.920
Where selection acts, changes depart from the neutral count.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.180
Purifying selection can make them lag; positive selection can make them run ahead.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.360
So how could you catch that in a real gene?

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.690
Here is the trick.

5
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.510
Find two kinds of change in the same gene that neutrality says should behave alike.

6
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:29.750
If they part ways by more than chance allows, selection may be at work.

7
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:33.783
Take a coding gene, read three letters at a time.

8
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:38.563
The genetic code has several codons for most amino acids.

9
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:41.943
So some changes keep the amino acid.

10
00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:44.203
These are synonymous.

11
00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:48.027
Other changes alter the amino acid.

12
00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:52.027
These are nonsynonymous, or replacement, changes.

13
00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:57.707
If synonymous changes are neutral, they are a built-in control for the replacements.

14
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.310
Count each kind along a branch, per site where it could happen.

15
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.950
Call them dN and dS.

16
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.630
Their ratio is called omega.

17
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.920
Below one, replacements were fixed more slowly than silent changes.

18
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.920
That suggests purifying selection removed some.

19
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.320
Near one is consistent with neutrality.

20
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.000
Above one suggests positive selection.

21
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:28.283
A second test uses variation inside a species.

22
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:35.523
In 1991, John McDonald and Martin Kreitman applied it to the Adh gene of fruit

23
00:01:35.523 --> 00:01:39.483
flies. The gene makes alcohol dehydrogenase.

24
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.497
Sort every change into a table.

25
00:01:43.737 --> 00:01:45.797
The columns are replacement and synonymous.

26
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:49.357
One row holds variants within a species.

27
00:01:50.717 --> 00:01:53.237
The other holds differences fixed between species.

28
00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:59.797
Under neutrality, a variant within a species is a passing stage, on its way to

29
00:01:59.797 --> 00:02:05.817
fixation or loss. So both rows should hold the same ratio of replacement to

30
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:06.817
synonymous changes.

31
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.390
The variants row fills first, and it is mostly synonymous.

32
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.150
If replacements were neutral, what should the fixed row look like?

33
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:24.787
Neutrality predicts the same ratio.

34
00:02:26.127 --> 00:02:30.427
Instead, the fixed row held more replacements than the variants predicted.

35
00:02:31.827 --> 00:02:35.647
McDonald and Kreitman suggested this reflected adaptive fixation.

36
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:40.007
Here each kind of change is the control for the other.

37
00:02:41.207 --> 00:02:43.987
So this test needs no clock and no dated split.

38
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.960
Both tests are model inferences, and each model can fail.

39
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.440
A signal like this still needs further checks before it counts as adaptation.

40
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.660
Both tests ask the same question.

41
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.720
Did one kind of change keep pace with the neutral count?

42
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.560
A departure is a clue to selection, not proof.
